        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Reebok licenses its name rather than building treadmills, and the licensee has differed by
            territory and era — so there is no single manufacturer to call.
            <strong class="text-white">The compliance plate on your frame usually names who actually
            built it</strong>, and that photograph is the key to the entire parts problem.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most of what fails is generic and orderable by dimension; the board is the part that ends
            these machines. So
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">lubricate on schedule</a>,
            take the free donor machine when it appears, and when you replace it, buy a badge with a
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">manufacturer attached to it</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
